3. Task
For each AD component in scope: decide build, adapt, or acquire; choose the
minimum set of design models needed; identify objects; specify interfaces;
apply patterns only where a named problem exists; define the implementation
environment, configuration management, and licensing position; and trace every
element to a component and requirement.
4. Requirements
F1. DESIGN DEPTH. Per component, state the design detail level (sketch,
    structured document, or detailed model) and justify it from the PAR's
    criticality and process shape, not habit.

F2. BUY / BUILD / REUSE. Before designing any component from scratch, decide
    how it is obtained, at one of four reuse levels: abstraction (reuse a
    pattern), object (library), component (framework), or system (COTS).
    Record for each option the costs: search and assessment, testing in this
    environment, purchase, adaptation, and integration (especially conflicting
    assumptions). If a component is a configured COTS system, its design is
    its configuration; do not model it as new objects.

F3. MODEL SELECTION. Choose the minimum models that serve a reader: structural
    (classes, generalization, composition) and dynamic (sequence, state
    machine). State which models you did not produce and why. Check the
    modeling notation against the implementation language: UML class models
    suit statically typed object-oriented languages, help less with dynamic
    languages, and make no sense for configuring a packaged product.

F4. OBJECT IDENTIFICATION. Use at least two sources and cross-check them:
    grammatical analysis of the requirements, tangible domain entities, and
    scenario analysis. Check against the SMS domain model. Each class cites a
    requirement, SMS element, and component id. A class with none is
    DESIGN-PROPOSED and labeled.

F5. INTERFACES FIRST. Specify the interface of every inter-component boundary
    in the AD before component internals: operation names, parameters, return
    types, and failure behavior, with no data representation. One object may
    expose several interfaces. Interfaces must be unambiguous enough for
    parallel development. They must match the AD's interfaces; report any
    divergence.

F6. DYNAMIC MODELS. One sequence model per significant interaction, chosen by
    risk and ambiguity, not for exhaustive coverage. A state machine only for
    objects with non-trivial state; state which objects have none.

F7. PATTERNS. Apply a design pattern only where this design has a problem the
    pattern's stated problem matches. For each use cite: the pattern, the
    problem here, the solution applied, and the consequence accepted. Applying
    no pattern is an acceptable outcome. A pattern chosen because it is known
    is DESIGN-PROPOSED.

F8. IMPLEMENTATION ENVIRONMENT. State configuration management: version
    management, system integration (how builds are assembled), and problem
    tracking. State the host and target platforms, whether simulators are
    needed (for example embedded targets), and deployment decisions: each
    component's platform requirements, availability needs (redundancy), and
    which high-traffic components should be co-located.

F9. OPEN SOURCE AND LICENSING. Decide two questions separately: whether the
    product will use open-source components, and whether it will be developed
    as open source. For each reused open-source component record its license
    and the obligation it creates (reciprocal licenses can require publishing
    your source; permissive ones may not), and test it against
    `{{LICENSING_CONTEXT}}`. An unknown license makes the reuse BLOCKED, not
    assumed fine.

F10. TRACEABILITY. Every design element cites an AD component id and an SRS
     requirement id. Every requirement the AD allocated reaches at least one
     design element or is listed unrealized with a reason.

F11. REVISION RECORD. Design is iterative. Record each backtrack made during
     this run, what discovery caused it, and which upstream artifact it
     implicates.

F12. INDEX. Emit the block in Section 7.

N1. Minimal models; N2. no production code; N3. domain neutrality, and if the
implementation is not object-oriented, state the equivalent decomposition; N4.
no element rendered as required when its basis is design-proposed.
5. Constraints
- Do not alter the architecture. Raise `COMPONENT_BOUNDARY_WRONG` or
  `ARCHITECTURE_VIOLATION` instead.
- Do not design a component from scratch before the F2 reuse decision exists.
- Do not apply a pattern, model, or class without a driver; label it
  DESIGN-PROPOSED.
- Do not assume a reused component's license is compatible.
- Do not put data representation in an interface.
- Do not produce all available UML diagram types by default.
- Do not copy SMS domain classes into design classes without a stated reason.
- On re-entry, revise the prior DIP and report the diff.
- If SRS, AD, or SMS is missing or has `halt: true`, emit HALT naming it.
6. Process
1. Read the AD component list and SRS provenance first. 2. Make the F2 reuse
decision per component before any modeling. 3. Pick models (F3), then identify
objects (F4) from at least two sources, then specify interfaces (F5), then
dynamic models (F6), then patterns (F7); tag basis as each element is made,
not afterward. 4. Define environment and licensing (F8, F9). 5. Trace (F10).
6. Self-critique: any untraced element, any unlabeled design-proposed element,
any unchecked license, any model produced out of habit. 7. Adversarial pass:
name the single element most likely a habitual choice with a justification
written afterward, and state whether you verified that. No mid-run questions;
record gaps in F11.
HITL variant: after step 2, present the reuse decisions alone and pause.
7. Output Format
Document "Design and Implementation Plan — <system>": 0 Preface/diff;
1 Design Depth; 2 Buy/Build/Reuse; 3 Models Selected and Omitted;
4 Objects; 5 Interfaces; 6 Dynamic Models; 7 Patterns; 8 Environment and
Configuration Management; 9 Open Source and Licensing; 10 Traceability;
11 Revision Record; 12 Index.
Each diagram is Mermaid/PlantUML text followed by | Element | Component id | Cites | Basis |.

Section 12 YAML:
  dip_version: 1
  project_id: <slug>   # matches PAR, SRS, SMS, AD
  components:
    - id: <C#>
      depth: sketch | document | detailed
      obtained: build | adapt | acquire
      reuse_level: abstraction | object | component | system | none
      license: <string> | unknown | n/a
  elements:
    - name: <string>
      kind: class | interface | pattern | model | component
      component: <C#>
      cites: [<requirement ids>]
      basis: driven | contingent | design-proposed
  models_omitted: [<kind + reason>]
  unrealized_requirements: [<ids + reason>]
  raises_upstream: [{signal, to, detail}]
  halt: false
On halt: only this YAML with `halt: true` and a `missing` list.
8. Verification
V1 Sections 0-12 present and YAML valid; project_id matches upstream artifacts.
V2 Every component has an F2 obtained-decision recorded before its design.
V3 Every element has cites and basis; none blank.
V4 Every AD-allocated requirement is realized or listed unrealized.
V5 Every interface contains no data representation and matches the AD.
V6 Every pattern entry has problem-here, solution, and consequence.
V7 Every reused component has a license or is marked blocked.
V8 Models_omitted is non-empty or justified as all-selected.
V9 Spot-check five `driven` elements against the SRS index; a miss makes all
   labels suspect.
V10 Adversarial pass names a specific element.
V11 (generalization) Run on a COTS-configuration system, a from-scratch
    object-oriented system, and a dynamic-language service: model and depth
    choices must differ materially.
V12 (generalization) Run with a reciprocal-licensed dependency and a
    proprietary distribution context: the DIP must surface the conflict and
    raise `REUSE_INFEASIBLE` or block the reuse, not silently adopt it.
Library Notes
Prompt element	Source slides
F1, F3	4 (design may live only in a sketch; UML fit to language), 14 (13 UML types; minimize)
F2	4 (buy or build, COTS), 25–26 (four reuse levels; benefits and costs)
F4	12–13 (grammatical, tangible entity, scenario sources)
F5	17 (interfaces enable parallel design; no data representation)
F6	15–16 (one sequence model per interaction; not every object needs a state model)
F7	19–22 (pattern elements; benefits; need for experience)
F8	24, 27–28 (configuration management, host-target, deployment)
F9	30–31 (open source decisions; GPL, LGPL, BSD)

Design decisions to defend. F2 comes before modeling because the source treats buy-or-build as an early decision, and designing first biases toward building. F7 allows "no pattern" because the source says patterns need experience and are hard to match in advance; forcing one would invent a problem. F9 treats an unknown license as BLOCKED so reuse cannot quietly outrun legal fit.

Known risks. Backfilled justification for habitual patterns; UML produced for every component regardless of language (watch V11); license checks that assert compatibility without evidence (V12); SMS classes copied directly into design (F4 cross-check). REUSE_INFEASIBLE is inert until CH6 declares it.